  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nSurface high pressure has regained control of the upper Ohio\nRiver Valley and will combine with gradual height rises aloft\nensure more days without rain. Temperature will hold around 5\ndegrees above normal again on Saturday despite cool advection \nbefore rising well above normal (10+ degrees higher than the \ndaily average) starting Sunday.\n\nInitial rain chances coincided with the eastward movement of a\ntrough currently entering the Central Plains, but ensemble\nguidance continues to trend it towards a more muted/subtle wave\nthat sags south/southwest of the region for the Monday/Tuesday\nperiod. Any rain chances those days (mainly Tuesday) would be\nwith a nominal warm front lifting through the region as the\nridge re-amplifies in conjunction with a secondary trough\ndeepening over the Intermountain West. Rain chances increase\nWednesday and beyond with that trough moving eastward, but\ncluster analysis shows a large variance in its\nshape/timing/depth that could portend further delays in the\nreturn of rain. Regardless, concerns about impactful weather is\nvery low as ensemble mean RUNTIME accumulation (not just\n24-hour, but full 7-day accumulation) shows only 10-30% \nprobability of exceeding 1\" around the area. So even if the rain\nfinally does arrive, it is unlikely to pose much threat.",
